\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

Degeneracy (12.2--67.9\%) and LP-quality alignment ($\rho$ 0.043--0.667) constrain aggregation; temperature disentangles these (\S\ref{sec:scaling}; Table~\ref{tab:t_degeneracy}).
Across four frontier models, degeneracy and the selection gap are negatively correlated (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}), consistent with the bound (\S\ref{sec:theory}).

\paragraph{Pretrained vs.\ finetuned.}
On CoNLL, pretrained MV captures ${\sim}98$\% of oracle headroom (degeneracy $36$\%) versus ${<}2$\% finetuned ($57$--$60$\%); 7/8 entity types flip MV direction after finetuning (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}, \ref{app:pertype}).

\paragraph{From diagnosis to practice.}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}
Entity-level construction bypasses both failure mechanisms when degeneracy ${<}22$\% ($p{<}0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}); even where MV turns positive at $N{\geq}32$, LP-based methods dominate at $8{\times}$ less cost (Figure~\ref{fig:n_scaling}).
These gains ($+0.3$--$1.4$\,pp) are diagnostic: aggregation underperforms above $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, with near-zero $\alpha$ as the primary bottleneck \cite{kadavath2022language, xiong2024verbalized}.
